            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> so when we left the current position, a cursor would be left behind\n\ndon't know how to make sense of that, since every motion \"leaves the current position\".\n\n\n\n\n\n> Perhaps the main cursor actually _should_ be a multicursor, and when reading from the nvim.multicursor namespace, it would be included with some data (`{ primary = true }`)\n\nit's enticing. but the first problem is that the primary cursor is window-relative: every window showing the same buffer, has its own cursor. whereas multicursors are buffer-relative.\n\nalso it ends up requiring special handling everywhere, and i'm not aware of any use-cases where it simplifies things enough to offset that.",
